"""
Wind-driven convection film coefficient on an external wall surface.

Ported from `ShipSIM.Components.Others.HeatTransfer.ConvectionFactors.ExternalConvection`.
Nusselt–Jürges, with the windward/leeward correction the building-services literature
applies to it:

```math
h_e = W \\left(7.13 \\, u^{0.78} + 5.35 \\, e^{-0.6 u}\\right)
\\qquad W = \\begin{cases} 1 & \\theta \\le 100° \\\\ 0.5 & \\theta > 100° \\end{cases}
```

where `u` is the wind speed and `θ` the angle between the direction the wind travels and
the inward surface normal `-Norm` — so `θ` near 0 is wind blowing square onto the
surface, and a surface in the lee sees half the coefficient.

`Norm` is the outward surface normal, `{0, -1, 0}` (due south, in the x = east,
y = north, z = up world frame) by default. The wind vector follows the convention the
rest of the port uses, pointing the way the wind travels: `Environment` and
`VariableEnvironment` give a direction the wind comes *from*, so a wind vector built from
them is `u · {-sin(dir), -cos(dir), 0}`. It arrives as three scalar inputs, as the other
vector-valued components here do (`IrradiationOnPlane`); Dyad has no array connectors.

Deviations from upstream, both numerical:

- The windward test compares cosines rather than angles, and the angle itself is not
  computed. `acos` is monotone decreasing, so `θ > 100°` and `cos θ < cos 100°` select the
  same surfaces; but `acos` has an infinite derivative at ±1, which is exactly the common
  case of a wind blowing square onto a wall. `cos_theta` is the diagnostic instead.
- The wind speed is the regularised magnitude `sqrt(|u|² + u_reg²)`, and everything —
  the 0.78 power, the exponential and the direction cosine — is built on that one
  quantity. Written literally the component has two singularities at rest, and a model
  whose wind *passes through* zero hits both. A ship leaving harbour does exactly that.

  `sqrt(u_x² + u_y² + u_z²)` has no gradient at the origin: `∂|u|/∂u_y` is `u_y/|u|`,
  which is `0/0` when the wind is exactly zero, so the Jacobian picks up a NaN at `t = 0`.
  And `|u|^0.78` has derivative `0.78|u|^(-0.22)`, which a `max(|u|, tiny)` floor does not
  bound — it jumps to about 2400 W/(m²·K) per m/s at `tiny = 1e-12` the instant the wind
  moves. Either is enough to drive the integrator's step below floating-point epsilon at
  `t = 0` once the coefficient feeds a wall face with no capacitance, as it does in a
  `ShipCompartment`; the model is reported as unstable with no other clue. A *steady* wind
  of any speed, zero included, hides both, because nothing is differentiated with respect
  to it.

  The cosine is separately `0/0` at rest; the same regularised magnitude makes it 0 there,
  so the surface reads as windward (`W = 1`) — which is what upstream's
  `angle_btw_vectors` returns for a zero-length vector.

  The cost is 0.1 % on the still-air coefficient: 5.355 rather than 5.350 W/(m²·K). Above
  0.01 m/s the regularisation is below one part in 10⁴.
"""
component ExternalConvection
  extends ConvectionBaseClass
  "Wind vector, east component — points the way the wind travels [m/s]"
  WindVector_x = RealInput()
  "Wind vector, north component [m/s]"
  WindVector_y = RealInput()
  "Wind vector, up component [m/s]"
  WindVector_z = RealInput()

  "Wall surface area [m²]"
  parameter Surf::Area
  "Outward surface normal, pointing away from the wall"
  parameter Norm::Real[3] = [0, -1, 0]

  "Regularisation speed: smooths the 0.78 power and the direction cosine at rest [m/s]"
  parameter u_reg::Real = 1e-4

  "cos 100°, the windward/leeward threshold"
  final parameter cos_theta_max::Real = -0.17364817766693041
  final parameter norm_len::Real = sqrt(Norm[1] ^ 2 + Norm[2] ^ 2 + Norm[3] ^ 2)

  "Wind speed, regularised by u_reg so it is differentiable at rest [m/s]"
  variable WindSpeed::Real
  "Cosine of the angle between the wind direction and the inward surface normal"
  variable cos_theta::Real
  "Windward/leeward factor"
  variable W::Real
relations
  A = Surf

  WindSpeed = sqrt(WindVector_x ^ 2 + WindVector_y ^ 2 + WindVector_z ^ 2 + u_reg ^ 2)

  cos_theta = -(WindVector_x * Norm[1] + WindVector_y * Norm[2] + WindVector_z * Norm[3])
              / (WindSpeed * norm_len)

  W = ifelse(cos_theta < cos_theta_max, 0.5, 1)

  h = W * (7.13 * WindSpeed ^ 0.78 + 5.35 * exp(-0.6 * WindSpeed))
end
